  "candidate_0018": {
    "reason": "The assertion conflates a specific technique with the entire category. Regularization is a broad class of methods (e.g., dropout, early stopping, data augmentation) that do not employ weight decay; weight decay is merely one instance. The literal text implies all regularization employs weight decay, which is factually false.",
    "counterexample": "Dropout is a widely used regularization technique in neural networks that operates by randomly zeroing out units during training and does not involve adding an L2 penalty (weight decay) to the loss function.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  },
  "candidate_0020": {
    "reason": "The assertion presents a mechanism as a universal guarantee. While weight decay often improves generalization, it is not an absolute rule; in specific scenarios (e.g., underfitting or when the optimal solution requires large weights), penalizing large weights can degrade performance. The claim lacks necessary conditions regarding the data distribution and model capacity.",
    "counterexample": "In a task where the true underlying function has high-frequency components requiring large weight magnitudes to represent accurately, applying strong weight decay forces the model into an overly smooth solution, resulting in higher generalization error compared to no regularization or other forms.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  }
}
